<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kinzua Dam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kinzua Dam is a flood-control dam on the Allegheny River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, completed in 1965. Its reservoir flooded about 10,000 acres of the Seneca Nation's Allegany land and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornplanter">Cornplanter</a> Grant, forcing some 600 Seneca to leave their homes. The project broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-canandaigua">Treaty of Canandaigua</a> of 1794 and remains a bitter memory for the Seneca.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kinzua-dam.jpg" alt="Kinzua Dam" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Niagara · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kinzua_Dam,_July_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
